{"abstract":"Recent advances in archaeogenomics have granted access to previously unavailable biological information with the potential to further our understanding of past social dynamics at a range of scales. However, to properly integrate these data within archaeological narratives, new methodological and theoretical tools are required. Effort must be put into finding new methods for weaving together different datasets where material culture and archaeogenomic data are both constitutive elements. This is true on a small scale, when we study relationships at the individual level, and at a larger scale when we deal with social and population dynamics. Specifically, in the study of kinship systems, it is essential to contextualize and make sense of biological relatedness through social relations, which, in archaeology, is achieved by using material culture as a proxy. In this paper, we propose a Network Science framework to integrate archaeogenomic data and material culture at an intra-site scale to study biological relatedness and social organization at the Neolithic site of Çatalhöyük. Methodologically, we propose the use of network variance to investigate the association between biological relatedness and material culture within networks of houses. This approach allows us to observe how material culture similarity between buildings is associated with biological relationships between individuals and how biogenetic ties concentrate at specific localities on site.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Mazzucato, Camilla","Coscia, Michele","Küçükakdağ Doğu, Ayça","Haddow, Scott D.","Kılıç, Muhammed Sıddık","Yüncü, Eren","Somel, Mehmet"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-799574"],"filters":{"added":"2025-04","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, Middle East","OECD"],"country":["Turkey"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Middle East Technical University"],"keyword":["Kinship","Neolithic","Network science","Southwest Asia","aDNA","Çatalhöyük"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a network of mutualities of being socio material archaeological networks and biological ties at çatalhöyük","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"http://www.scopus.com/inward/record.url?scp=85216197254&partnerID=8YFLogxK","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-799574","identifier":[{"id":"85216197254","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-5b543754-c14f-4d40-873d-906f48bfc764"],"keyword":["ADNA","Kinship","Neolithic","Network Science","Network variance","Southwest Asia","Çatalhöyük"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-46EEEB85C4353352CC3BD5E69FC64FFF"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Humanities, Department of Cross-Cultural and Regional Studies"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"tr","key":"middle east technical university","loc":["Turkey","Asia, Middle East","OECD"],"name":"Middle East Technical University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Camilla","key":"mazzucatopau","last":"Mazzucato","locid":"8ebcb8c4-14cc-4855-b5fd-74a11ce35479","name":"Mazzucato, Camilla","orcid":"0000-0002-4168-0773","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Michele","key":"cosciapau","last":"Coscia","name":"Coscia, Michele","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Ayça","key":"küçükakdağdoğupau","last":"Küçükakdağ Doğu","name":"Küçükakdağ Doğu, Ayça","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","country":"dk","first":"Scott","key":"haddowpau","last":"Haddow","locid":"ee55470b-6eec-4060-8ca7-826184d96ea4","name":"Haddow, Scott D.","name_org":"Haddow, Scott","orcid":"0000-0002-3970-7447","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Muhammed Sıddık","key":"kılıçpau","last":"Kılıç","name":"Kılıç, Muhammed Sıddık","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Eren","key":"yüncüpau","last":"Yüncü","name":"Yüncü, Eren","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Mehmet","key":"somelpau","last":"Somel","name":"Somel, Mehmet","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s10816-024-09692-3","issn":["10725369","15737764"],"issn_org":["10725369"],"issue":"1","journal":"Journal of Archaeological Method and Theory","paperid":"25","status":"p","title":"Journal of Archaeological Method and Theory","volume":"32"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85216197254"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-5b543754-c14f-4d40-873d-906f48bfc764"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"“A Network of Mutualities of Being” : Socio-material Archaeological Networks and Biological Ties at Çatalhöyük","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-799574","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Professional sports are a cultural activity beloved by many, and a global hundred-billion-dollar industry. In this paper, we investigate the trends of match outcome predictability, assuming that the public is more interested in an event if there is some uncertainty about who will win. We reproduce previous methodology focused on soccer and we expand it by analyzing more than 300,000 matches in the 1996-2023 period from nine disciplines, to identify which disciplines are getting more/less predictable over time. We investigate the home advantage effect, since it can affect outcome predictability and it has been impacted by the COVID-19 pandemic. Going beyond previous work, we estimate which sport management model – between the egalitarian one popular in North America and the rich-get-richer used in Europe – leads to more uncertain outcomes. Our results show that there is no generalized trend in predictability across sport disciplines, that home advantage has been decreasing independently from the pandemic, and that sports managed with the egalitarian North American approach tend to be less predictable. We base our result on a predictive model that ranks team by analyzing the directed network of who-beats-whom, where the most central teams in the network are expected to be the best performing ones. Our results are robust to the measure we use for the prediction.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Coscia, Michele"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-754069"],"filters":{"added":"2024-09","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Data science","Sports","Sports analysis"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"which sport is becoming more predictable a cross discipline analysis of predictability in team sports","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1140/epjds/s13688-024-00448-3","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-754069","identifier":[{"id":"85183378332","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-3bd939c8-13af-47dc-bd21-6582916f740c"],"keyword":["data science","sports","sports analysis"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-5511E78CA76E4FC38CE570ED8DD6A607"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1140/epjds/s13688-024-00448-3","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michele","key":"cosciapau","last":"Coscia","locid":"90b501a9-4d44-460b-b193-4d7d524bb21b","name":"Coscia, Michele","orcid":"0000-0001-5984-5137","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1140/epjds/s13688-024-00448-3","issn":["21931127"],"journal":"EPJ Data Science","pages":"1-20","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"EPJ Data Science"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85183378332"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-3bd939c8-13af-47dc-bd21-6582916f740c"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Which sport is becoming more predictable? A cross-discipline analysis of predictability in team sports","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-754069","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Wikipedia is a widely used tool people use to gather knowledge about the world, causing it to have a vast impact on the way individuals perceive the reality they live in. It is then of paramount importance that the picture of the world Wikipedia provides is accurate. We cannot afford such an important tool to eschew inclusiveness or a fair representation of reality: an inaccurate picture of the world in such a tool can be used to claim unjust and unfair positions such as that women are inferior to men as if they were facts, because they are enshrined on an encyclopedia. In this paper, we study issues of fair gender representations for people in history noted by multiple language editions of Wikipedia: are women underrepresented on Wikipedia? We do so via a combination of natural language processing and network science. Our results indicate that there is indeed a higher bar for women to have their own biographical page on Wikipedia: women are only included when they have more significant connections than men to the rest of the network. There are visible effects of the initiatives Wikipedia is taking to fix this issue, showing that the gap is narrowing, which validates our interpretation of the data.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Krivaa, Lea","Coscia, Michele"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-754068"],"filters":{"added":"2024-09","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Gender bias","History","Network analysis","Networks","Wikipedia"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"traces of unequal entry requirement for illustrious people on wikipedia based on their gender","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/106187822/main.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-754068","identifier":[{"id":"85196317669","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-385c3c05-30a7-41f2-b9ad-7c0553dd8c60"],"keyword":["gender bias","history","network analysis","networks","wikipedia"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-9AD2EF50B79051AE80B1965B01BED855"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/106187822/main.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Lea","key":"krivaapau","last":"Krivaa","name":"Krivaa, Lea","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michele","key":"cosciapau","last":"Coscia","locid":"90b501a9-4d44-460b-b193-4d7d524bb21b","name":"Coscia, Michele","orcid":"0000-0001-5984-5137","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["02195259","17936802"],"issn_org":["02195259"],"journal":"Advances in Complex Systems","pages":"1-17","status":"p","title":"Advances in Complex Systems"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85196317669"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-385c3c05-30a7-41f2-b9ad-7c0553dd8c60"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Traces Of Unequal Entry Requirement For Illustrious People On Wikipedia Based On Their Gender","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-754068","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"An intensely debated topic is whether political polarization on social media is on the rise. We can investigate this question only if we can quantify polarization, by taking into account how extreme the opinions of the people are, how much they organize into echo chambers, and how these echo chambers organize in the network. Current polarization estimates are insensitive to at least one of these factors: they cannot conclusively clarify the opening question. Here, we propose a measure of ideological polarization which can capture the factors we listed. The measure is based on the Generalized Euclidean (GE) distance, which estimates the distance between two vectors on a network, e.g., representing people’s opinion. This measure can fill the methodological gap left by the state of the art, and leads to useful insights when applied to real-world debates happening on social media and to data from the US Congress.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Hohmann, Marilena","Devriendt, Karel","Coscia, Michele"],"cuid":["S02-099.68-673002"],"filters":{"added":"2023-07","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","The Alan Turing Institute","University of Oxford"],"keyword":["Complex networks","Political polarization","Social media"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci","soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"quantifying ideological polarization on a network using generalized euclidean distance","src":["itu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abq2044","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-099.68-673002","identifier":[{"id":"36857460","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC9977176","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"000948537800019","type":"wos"},{"id":"85149331265","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-5dc49311-eb55-4609-8b5e-fab460ce130a","ku-1259841b-f982-4b13-8a56-09f7f8af0653"],"keyword":["Complex networks","Political polarization","Social media"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology","Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-829D4C0292189055BE85649A363A1EDF"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/359791969/sciadv.abq2044.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/94904159/polarization.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abq2044","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Social Sciences, Faculty of Social Sciences, shared expenditure, Social Data Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"university of oxford","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Oxford","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"gb","key":"the alan turing institute","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"The Alan Turing Institute","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Marilena","key":"hohmannpau","last":"Hohmann","locid":"712198cd-b79d-4269-a6a9-a04674be727f","name":"Hohmann, Marilena","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Karel","key":"devriendtpau","last":"Devriendt","name":"Devriendt, Karel","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Michele","key":"cosciapau","last":"Coscia","locid":"90b501a9-4d44-460b-b193-4d7d524bb21b","name":"Coscia, Michele","orcid":"0000-0001-5984-5137","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["36857460"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1126/sciadv.abq2044","issn":["23752548"],"issue":"9","journal":"Science Advances","paperid":"eabq2044","status":"p","title":"Science Advances","volume":"9"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85149331265"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-5dc49311-eb55-4609-8b5e-fab460ce130a","ku-1259841b-f982-4b13-8a56-09f7f8af0653"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Quantifying Ideological Polarization on a Network Using Generalized Euclidean Distance","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-099.68-673002","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Many people use social media as a primary information source, but their questionable reliability has pushed platforms to contain misinformation via crowdsourced flagging systems. Such systems, however, assume that users are impartial arbiters of truth. This assumption might be unwarranted, as users might be influenced by their own political biases and tolerance for opposing points of view, besides considering the truth value of a news item. In this paper we simulate a scenario in which users on one side of the polarity spectrum have different tolerance levels for the opinions of the other side. We create a model based on some assumptions about online news consumption, including echo chambers, selective exposure, and confirmation bias. A consequence of such a model is that news sources on the opposite side of the intolerant users attract more flags. We extend the base model in two ways: (i) by allowing news sources to find the path of least resistance that leads to a minimization of backlash, and (ii) by allowing users to change their tolerance level in response to a perceived lower tolerance from users on the other side of the spectrum. With these extensions, in the model we see that intolerance is attractive: news sources are nudged to move their polarity to the side of the intolerant users. Such a model does not support high-tolerance regimes: these regimes are out of equilibrium and will converge towards empirically-supported low-tolerance states under the assumption of partisan but rational users.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Westermann, Camilla Jung","Coscia, Michele"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383162"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Agent based model","Network analysis","Polarization","Social media"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a potential mechanism for low tolerance feedback loops in social media flagging systems","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0268270","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383162","identifier":[{"id":"85130788070","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-813a0260-dbbb-42b1-8d42-e4626ecfcf38"],"keyword":["agent-based model","network analysis","polarization","social media"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-9EFCC8C5739B33C93AA7A3E7A38E0A3F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0268270","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86778494/pone.0268270.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Camilla Jung","key":"westermannpau","last":"Westermann","name":"Westermann, Camilla Jung","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michele","key":"cosciapau","last":"Coscia","locid":"90b501a9-4d44-460b-b193-4d7d524bb21b","name":"Coscia, Michele","orcid":"0000-0001-5984-5137","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1371/journal.pone.0268270","issn":["19326203"],"journal":"PLOS ONE","publisher":"Public Library of Science","status":"p","title":"PLOS ONE"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85130788070"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-813a0260-dbbb-42b1-8d42-e4626ecfcf38"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"A potential mechanism for low tolerance feedback loops in social media flagging systems","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383162","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Social media represent an important source of news for many users. They are, however, affected by misinformation and they might be playing a role in the growth of political polarization. In this paper, we create an agent based model to investigate how policing content and backlash on social media (i.e. conflict) can lead to an increase in polarization for both users and news sources. Our model is an advancement over previously proposed models because it allows us to study the polarization of both users and news sources, the evolution of the audience connections between users and sources, and it makes more realistic assumptions about the starting conditions of the system. We find that the tendency of users and sources to avoid policing, backlash and conflict in general can increase polarization online. Specifically polarization comes from the ease of sharing political posts, intolerance for opposing points of view causing backlash and policing, and volatility in changing one’s opinion when faced with new information. On the other hand, it seems that the integrity of a news source in trying to resist the backlash and policing has little effect.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Coscia, Michele","Rossi, Luca"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384617"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Agent based model","Misinformation","Online conflict","Political polarization","Social media"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"how minimizing conflicts could lead to polarization on social media an agent based model investigation","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0263184","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384617","identifier":[{"id":"85123604649","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-e350f1b1-e3c2-461e-94c6-acfa6115af8e"],"keyword":["Agent-based model","Misinformation","Online conflict","Political polarization","Social media"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-4D4C503CBD8568CFAEC5C93500D33728"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0263184","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0263184","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michele","key":"cosciapau","last":"Coscia","locid":"90b501a9-4d44-460b-b193-4d7d524bb21b","name":"Coscia, Michele","orcid":"0000-0001-5984-5137","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Luca","key":"rossipau","last":"Rossi","locid":"1b45e3f5-b8cd-4c0d-a444-2901304f76ee","name":"Rossi, Luca","orcid":"0000-0002-3629-2039","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1371/journal.pone.0263184","issn":["19326203"],"issue":"1","journal":"PLOS ONE","publisher":"Public Library of Science","status":"p","title":"PLOS ONE","volume":"17"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85123604649"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-e350f1b1-e3c2-461e-94c6-acfa6115af8e"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"How minimizing conflicts could lead to polarization on social media: An agent-based model investigation","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384617","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Information has major consequences for democracy and society. It is important to understand what factors favor its diffusion. The impact of the content of a message on its likelihood of going viral is poorly understood. Some studies say originality is important for a message not to be overlooked. Others give more relevance to paratextual elements—network centrality, timing, human cognitive limits. Here we propose that originality and centrality interact in a nontrivial way, which might explain why originality by itself is not a good predictor of success. We collected data from Reddit on users sharing hyperlinks. We estimated the originality of each post title and the centrality of the website hosting the shared link. We show that the interaction effect exists: if users share content from a central website, originality no longer increases the odds of receiving at least one upvote. The same is not true for the odds of becoming one of the top 10% scoring posts. We show that originality is concentrated in the domain network: domains in the core of the network produce more original content. Our results imply that research on online information virality needs to take into account the nontrivial interaction between originality and prominence.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Coscia, Michele","Vandeweerdt, Clara"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-673003"],"filters":{"added":"2023-07","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Network analysis","memetics"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci","soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"posts on central websites need less originality to be noticed","src":["itu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-022-19433-9","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-673003","identifier":[{"id":"85138127794","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC9464218","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"36088489","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["itu-64efd9e2-314c-476f-92da-8cd3c0802643","ku-52dd99f9-674a-4b6a-a927-6adb85938f99"],"keyword":["memetics","network analysis"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology","Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-7141E6AE2468B93DCF0831D37F86178F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"rem","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-022-19433-9","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-022-19433-9","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/347002536/s41598_022_19433_9.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Social Sciences, Department of Political Science, Danish and Comparative Politics – Behavior, Institutions, and Methods","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Social Sciences, Department of Political Science, Department of Political Science","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Social Sciences, Department of Political Science, Research Group on Climate, Environment and Change (REGROUP)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michele","key":"cosciapau","last":"Coscia","locid":"90b501a9-4d44-460b-b193-4d7d524bb21b","name":"Coscia, Michele","orcid":"0000-0001-5984-5137","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Clara","key":"vandeweerdtpau","last":"Vandeweerdt","locid":"c7f288d6-0ac0-4741-a571-2a858e03dce1","name":"Vandeweerdt, Clara","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["36088489"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/s41598-022-19433-9","issn":["20452322"],"issue":"1","journal":"Scientific Reports","paperid":"15265","status":"p","title":"Scientific Reports","volume":"12"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85138127794"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-64efd9e2-314c-476f-92da-8cd3c0802643","ku-52dd99f9-674a-4b6a-a927-6adb85938f99"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Posts on central websites need less originality to be noticed","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-673003","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Estimating the distance covered by a spreading event on a network can lead to a better understanding of epidemics, economic growth, and human behavior. There are many methods solving this problem – which has been called Node Vector Distance (NVD) – for single layer networks. However, many phenomena are better represented by multilayer networks: networks in which nodes can connect in qualitatively different ways. In this paper, we extend the literature by proposing an algorithm solving NVD for multilayer networks. We do so by adapting the Mahalanobis distance, incorporating the graph’s topology via the pseudoinverse of its Laplacian. Since this is a proper generalization of the Euclidean distance in a complex space defined by the topology of the graph, and that it works on multilayer networks, we call our measure the Multi Layer Generalized Euclidean (MLGE). In our experiments, we show that MLGE is intuitive, theoretically simpler than the alternatives, performs well in recovering infection parameters, and it is useful in specific case studies. MLGE requires solving a special case of the effective resistance on the graph, which has a high time complexity. However, this needs to be done only once per network. In the experiments, we show that MLGE can cache its most computationally-heavy parts, allowing it to solve hundreds of NVD problems on the same network with little to no additional runtime. MLGE is provided as a free open source tool, along with the data and the code necessary to replicate our results.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Coscia, Michele"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381824"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Complex networks","Multilayer networks"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"generalized euclidean measure to estimate distances on multilayer networks","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86666385/ge_ml.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381824","identifier":[{"id":"85141073214","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-250505ea-82fc-42e1-ae6d-7e046575051c"],"keyword":["complex networks","multilayer networks"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-9B683332B15579E5F5148A7C4F3C017B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86666385/ge_ml.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michele","key":"cosciapau","last":"Coscia","locid":"90b501a9-4d44-460b-b193-4d7d524bb21b","name":"Coscia, Michele","orcid":"0000-0001-5984-5137","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3529396","issn":["15564681","1556472X"],"issn_org":["15564681"],"journal":"ACM Transactions on Knowledge Discovery from Data","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"ACM Transactions on Knowledge Discovery from Data"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85141073214"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-250505ea-82fc-42e1-ae6d-7e046575051c"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Generalized Euclidean Measure to Estimate Distances on Multilayer Networks","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381824","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Complex networks are useful tools to understand propagation events like epidemics, word-of-mouth, adoption of habits and innovations. Estimating the correlation between two processes happening on the same network is therefore an important problem with a number of applications. However, at present there is no way to do so: current methods either correlate a network with itself, a single process with the network structure or calculate a network distance between two processes. In this article, we propose to extend the Pearson correlation coefficient to work on complex networks. Given two vectors, we define a function that uses the topology of the network to return a correlation coefficient. We show that our formulation is intuitive and returns the expected values in a number of scenarios. We also demonstrate how the classical the Pearson correlation coefficient is unable to do so. We conclude the article with two case studies, showcasing how our network correlation can facilitate tasks in social network analysis and economics. We provide examples of how we could use our network correlation to infer user characteristics from their activities on social media; and relationships between industrial products, under some assumptions as to what should make two exporting countries similar.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Coscia, Michele"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383111"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Complex networks","Correlation"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"pearson correlations on complex networks","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/comnet/cnab036","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383111","identifier":[{"id":"85119336763","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-7ded4acb-c877-403f-a8bb-4ecfc8e7522c"],"keyword":["complex networks","correlation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-07E5E2B3EBCB7F29B1E89267C5784AF8"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/comnet/cnab036","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86377701/nvd_corr.pdf","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michele","key":"cosciapau","last":"Coscia","locid":"90b501a9-4d44-460b-b193-4d7d524bb21b","name":"Coscia, Michele","orcid":"0000-0001-5984-5137","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1093/comnet/cnab036","issn":["20511310","20511329"],"issn_org":["20511310"],"issue":"6","journal":"Journal of Complex Networks","paperid":"6","publisher":"Oxford University Press","status":"p","title":"Journal of Complex Networks","volume":"9"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85119336763"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-7ded4acb-c877-403f-a8bb-4ecfc8e7522c"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Pearson correlations on complex networks","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383111","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"With the growth of social media, Twitter has become one of the most popularly used microblogging communication platforms between people. Due to the wide preference of Twitter, popular issues in public, events like local or global news and daily life stories can immediately publish on Twitter. Thus, a substantial number of hot topics are created by Twitter users in real-time. These topics can exhibit every incident of everyday life. Therefore, detection of hot topics can be used in many applications such as observing public judgment, product recommendation, and incidence detection. In this paper, we propose a method for detecting Twitter hot topics and evaluate the effect of multi-relations such as retweets and hashtags on hot topics. The dataset was generated by fetching tweets for a certain time and location by using GetOldTweets3 API. Then using the LDA topic modeling algorithm the hot topics were identified for each multi relation. Finally, the effect of each relation is described by using the coherence scores)","author_count":7,"contributors":["Zirbilek, Nadir Emre","Erakin, Mustafa","Özyer, Tansel","Alhajj, Reda"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-619508"],"event":{"acronym":"ASONAM","end":"2021-11-08","place":"Virtual, Online, nl","start":"2021-11-08","title":"13th IEEE/ACM International Conference on Advances in Social Networks Analysis and Mining, ASONAM 2021","year":"2021"},"filters":{"added":"2022-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Asia, Middle East","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Alhajj, Reda"],"country":["Canada","Turkey"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Istanbul Medipol University","TOBB University of Economics and Technology","University of Calgary"],"keyword":["Social media"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"hot topic detection and evaluation of multi relation effects","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85124380874","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-619508","identifier":[{"id":"85124380874","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-a1d2c21e-1996-42e0-ae39-7e7e588b9ad2"],"keyword":["hot topic detection","multi-relations","social media","tweeter"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-C5CD9FA36DBC889A61B654157AFD0812"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"ca","key":"university of calgary","loc":["Canada","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Calgary","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"tr","key":"tobb university of economics and technology","loc":["Turkey","Asia, Middle East","OECD"],"name":"TOBB University of Economics and Technology","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"tr","key":"istanbul medipol university","loc":["Turkey","Asia, Middle East","OECD"],"name":"Istanbul Medipol University","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Engineering, The Maersk Mc-Kinney Moller Institute","SDU, The Faculty of Engineering, The Maersk Mc-Kinney Moller Institute, SDU Health Informatics and Technology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nadir Emre","key":"zirbilekpau","last":"Zirbilek","name":"Zirbilek, Nadir Emre","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Mustafa","key":"erakinpau","last":"Erakin","name":"Erakin, Mustafa","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Tansel","key":"özyerpau","last":"Özyer","name":"Özyer, Tansel","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Reda","key":"alhajjpau","last":"Alhajj","locid":"1bf434d6-4241-4d51-9b4f-8537145d5938","name":"Alhajj, Reda","orcid":"0000-0001-6657-9738","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Michele","key":"cosciapedh","last":"Coscia","name":"Coscia, Michele","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Alfredo","key":"cuzzocreapedh","last":"Cuzzocrea","name":"Cuzzocrea, Alfredo","rank":6,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Kai","key":"shupedh","last":"Shu","name":"Shu, Kai","rank":7,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3487351.3490972","eisbn":["9781450391283"],"pages":"416-422","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 2021 IEEE/ACM International Conference on Advances in Social Networks Analysis and Mining, ASONAM 2021"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85124380874"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-a1d2c21e-1996-42e0-ae39-7e7e588b9ad2"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Hot topic detection and evaluation of multi-relation effects","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-619508","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Multiplex networks allow us to study a variety of complex systems where nodes connect to each other in multiple ways, for example friend, family, and co-worker relations in social networks. Link prediction is the branch of network analysis allowing us to forecast the future status of a network: which new connections are the most likely to appear in the future? In multiplex link prediction we also ask: of which type? Because this last question is unanswerable with classical link prediction, here we investigate the use of graph association rules to inform multiplex link prediction. We derive such rules by identifying all frequent patterns in a network via multiplex graph mining, and then score each unobserved link's likelihood by finding the occurrences of each rule in the original network. Association rules add new abilities to multiplex link prediction: to predict new node arrivals, to consider higher order structures with four or more nodes, and to be memory efficient. In our experiments, we show that, exploiting graph association rules, we are able to achieve a prediction performance close to an ideal ensemble classifier. Further, we perform a case study on a signed multiplex network, showing how graph association rules can provide valuable insights to extend social balance theory.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Coscia, Michele","Szell, Michael"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382528"],"event":{"acronym":"ICWSM","title":"International AAAI Conference on Web and Social Media"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Association rules","Graph mining","Multilayer networks","Multiplex networks"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"multiplex graph association rules for link prediction","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2008.08351","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382528","ids":["itu-5440fcc0-eca6-4560-b33b-9c2e77f0a59c"],"keyword":["association rules","graph mining","multilayer networks","multiplex networks"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-6A3AFD80FCC2A10B1CCA73660445B02B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2008.08351","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85885111/2008.08351.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michele","key":"cosciapau","last":"Coscia","locid":"90b501a9-4d44-460b-b193-4d7d524bb21b","name":"Coscia, Michele","orcid":"0000-0001-5984-5137","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"szellpau","last":"Szell","locid":"2ef641d0-bd79-425b-8b12-b58d74b993ae","name":"Szell, Michael","orcid":"0000-0003-3022-2483","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2008.08351","isbn":["9781577358695"],"pages":"129-139","publisher":"AAAI Press","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the Fifteenth International AAAI Conference on Web and Social Media, ICWSM 2021"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-5440fcc0-eca6-4560-b33b-9c2e77f0a59c"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Multiplex Graph Association Rules for Link Prediction","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382528","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"In this article, we propose a new method to perform topological network sampling. Topological network sampling is a process for extracting a subset of nodes and edges from a network, such that analyses on the sample provide results and conclusions comparable to the ones they would return if run on whole structure. We need network sampling because the largest online network datasets are accessed through low-throughput application programming interface (API) systems, rendering the collection of the whole network infeasible. Our method is inspired by the literature on network backboning, specifically the noise-corrected backbone. We select the next node to explore by following the edge we identify as the one providing the largest information gain, given the topology of the sample explored so far. We evaluate our method against the most commonly used sampling methods. We do so in a realistic framework, considering a wide array of network topologies, network analysis, and features of API systems. There is no method that can provide the best sample in all possible scenarios, thus in our results section, we show the cases in which our method performs best and the cases in which it performs worst. Overall, the noise-corrected network sampling performs well: it has the best rank average among the tested methods across a wide range of applications.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Coscia, Michele"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384615"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Social media","Social networks","network backboning","network sampling"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"noise corrected sampling of online social networks","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85885070/noise_corrected_sample.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384615","identifier":[{"id":"85103943902","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-e33f3c69-a577-420e-8d70-de703a867f3e"],"keyword":["network backboning","network sampling","social media","social networks"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-692E13BBB7BFC4362C9A906E629BE263"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85885070/noise_corrected_sample.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michele","key":"cosciapau","last":"Coscia","locid":"90b501a9-4d44-460b-b193-4d7d524bb21b","name":"Coscia, Michele","orcid":"0000-0001-5984-5137","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3434749","issn":["15564681","1556472X"],"issn_org":["15564681"],"issue":"2","journal":"ACM Transactions on Knowledge Discovery from Data","pages":"1-21","paperid":"29","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"ACM Transactions on Knowledge Discovery from Data","volume":"15"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85103943902"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-e33f3c69-a577-420e-8d70-de703a867f3e"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Noise Corrected Sampling of Online Social Networks","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384615","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"The available data of drugs and their targets has increased widely in recent years. Far from the traditional way of studying the drug-target interactions, we propose a network-based computational method to identify new targets for known drugs. In this study, the Stanford Biomedical Network Dataset Collection (BIOSNAP Datasets) is used. A network graph is constructed and analyzed to study the relationship between the drugs and their targets. Different centrality and similarity measures analyses are applied and predict new potential metabolism pathways for five drugs, namely (Wortmannin, Voacamine, Vancomycin, Dactinomycin and Arundic acid) through Cytochrome P450 3A4 enzyme in the liver. The application of network theory to the analysis of this dataset reveals a new significant approach. Finally the molecular docking is performed to confirm the results. Also, the importance of the presented method in drug discovery is highlighted/pointed out.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Albrijawi, M. Taleb","Ibrahim, Amrou Haj","Alhajj, Reda"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-631155"],"event":{"acronym":"ASONAM","end":"2021-11-08","place":"Virtual, Online, nl","start":"2021-11-08","title":"13th IEEE/ACM International Conference on Advances in Social Networks Analysis and Mining, ASONAM 2021","year":"2021"},"filters":{"added":"2022-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Asia, Middle East","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Albrijawi, M. Taleb"],"country":["Canada","Turkey"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Istanbul Medipol University","University of Calgary"],"keyword":["CYP3A4","Drug discovery"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"predictions of drug metabolism pathways through cyp 000000000003a000000000004 enzyme by analysing drug target interactions network graph","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85124392148","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-631155","identifier":[{"id":"85124392148","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-ca2b6de3-bff0-4327-9b45-d465afde62b1"],"keyword":["CYP3A4","cytochrome P450 enzyme family","drug discovery","drug-target interactions network","metabolism pathways","network graph"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-D86FD884A82717CC211AE67EEEA3F6F6"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"ca","key":"university of calgary","loc":["Canada","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Calgary","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"tr","key":"istanbul medipol university","loc":["Turkey","Asia, Middle East","OECD"],"name":"Istanbul Medipol University","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Engineering, The Maersk Mc-Kinney Moller Institute","SDU, The Faculty of Engineering, The Maersk Mc-Kinney Moller Institute, SDU Health Informatics and Technology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"M. Taleb","key":"albrijawipau","last":"Albrijawi","name":"Albrijawi, M. Taleb","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Amrou Haj","key":"ibrahimpau","last":"Ibrahim","name":"Ibrahim, Amrou Haj","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Reda","key":"alhajjpau","last":"Alhajj","locid":"1bf434d6-4241-4d51-9b4f-8537145d5938","name":"Alhajj, Reda","orcid":"0000-0001-6657-9738","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Michele","key":"cosciapedh","last":"Coscia","name":"Coscia, Michele","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Alfredo","key":"cuzzocreapedh","last":"Cuzzocrea","name":"Cuzzocrea, Alfredo","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Kai","key":"shupedh","last":"Shu","name":"Shu, Kai","rank":6,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3487351.3490959","eisbn":["9781450391283"],"pages":"495-501","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 2021 IEEE/ACM International Conference on Advances in Social Networks Analysis and Mining, ASONAM 2021"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85124392148"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-ca2b6de3-bff0-4327-9b45-d465afde62b1"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Predictions of drug metabolism pathways through CYP 3A4 enzyme by analysing drug-target interactions network graph","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-631155","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Nowadays, with the rapid increase in popularity of online social networks (OSNs), these platforms are realized as ideal places for spammers. Unfortunately, these spammers can easily publish malicious content, advertise phishing scams by taking advantage of OSNs. Therefore, effective identification and filtering of spam tweets will be beneficial to both OSNs and users. However, it is becoming increasingly difficult to check and eliminate spam tweets due to this great flow of posts. Motivated by these observations, in this paper we propose an approach for the detection of spam tweets using machine learning and effective preprocessing techniques. The approach proposes the advantages of the preprocessing and which of these preprocessing techniques are the most effective. To compare these techniques UtkML Twitter spam dataset is used in testing. After the most effective methods determined, the detection accuracy of the spam tweets will be better optimized by combining them. We have evaluated our solution with four different machine learning algorithms namely - Naïve Bayes Classifier, Neural Network, Logistic Regression and Support Vector Machine. With SVM Classifier, we are able to achieve an accuracy of 93.02%. Experimental results show that our approach can improve the performance of spam tweet classification effectively.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Kardaş, Berk","Bayar, Ismail Erdem","Özyer, Tansel","Alhajj, Reda"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-645535"],"event":{"acronym":"ASONAM","end":"2021-11-08","place":"Virtual, Online, nl","start":"2021-11-08","title":"13th IEEE/ACM International Conference on Advances in Social Networks Analysis and Mining, ASONAM 2021","year":"2021"},"filters":{"added":"2022-11","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Asia, Middle East","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Kardaş, Berk"],"country":["Canada","Turkey"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Istanbul Medipol University","TOBB University of Economics and Technology","University of Calgary"],"keyword":["Machine learning","Preprocessing","Social media","Spam detection","Twitter"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"detecting spam tweets using machine learning and effective preprocessing","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85124395764","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-645535","identifier":[{"id":"85124395764","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-fcc670a5-153a-44b1-aa4a-08a904a5b5e4"],"keyword":["Twitter","machine learning","preprocessing","social media","spam detection"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-4A8229E13C772AA2593697715B3A8FF9"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"ca","key":"university of calgary","loc":["Canada","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Calgary","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"tr","key":"tobb university of economics and technology","loc":["Turkey","Asia, Middle East","OECD"],"name":"TOBB University of Economics and Technology","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"tr","key":"istanbul medipol university","loc":["Turkey","Asia, Middle East","OECD"],"name":"Istanbul Medipol University","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Engineering, The Maersk Mc-Kinney Moller Institute","SDU, The Faculty of Engineering, The Maersk Mc-Kinney Moller Institute, SDU Health Informatics and Technology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Berk","key":"kardaşpau","last":"Kardaş","name":"Kardaş, Berk","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Ismail Erdem","key":"bayarpau","last":"Bayar","name":"Bayar, Ismail Erdem","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Tansel","key":"özyerpau","last":"Özyer","name":"Özyer, Tansel","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Reda","key":"alhajjpau","last":"Alhajj","locid":"1bf434d6-4241-4d51-9b4f-8537145d5938","name":"Alhajj, Reda","orcid":"0000-0001-6657-9738","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Michele","key":"cosciapedh","last":"Coscia","name":"Coscia, Michele","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Alfredo","key":"cuzzocreapedh","last":"Cuzzocrea","name":"Cuzzocrea, Alfredo","rank":6,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Kai","key":"shupedh","last":"Shu","name":"Shu, Kai","rank":7,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3487351.3490968","eisbn":["9781450391283"],"pages":"393-398","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 2021 IEEE/ACM International Conference on Advances in Social Networks Analysis and Mining, ASONAM 2021"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85124395764"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-fcc670a5-153a-44b1-aa4a-08a904a5b5e4"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Detecting spam tweets using machine learning and effective preprocessing","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-645535","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"We use aggregated and anonymized information based on international expenditures through corporate payment cards to map the network of global business travel. We combine this network with information on the industrial composition and export baskets of national economies. The business travel network helps to predict which economic activities will grow in a country, which new activities will develop and which old activities will be abandoned. In statistical terms, business travel has the most substantial impact among a range of bilateral relationships between countries, such as trade, foreign direct investments and migration. Moreover, our analysis suggests that this impact is causal: business travel from countries specializing in a specific industry causes growth in that economic activity in the destination country. Our interpretation of this is that business travel helps to diffuse knowledge, and we use our estimates to assess which countries contribute or benefit the most from the diffusion of knowledge through global business travel.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Coscia, Michele","Neffke, Frank","Hausmann, Ricardo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382998"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Harvard University"],"keyword":["Economics","Network analysis","Tacit knowledge","business travel"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"knowledge diffusion in the network of international business travel","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85337687/knowhow_index_NHB_cameraready.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382998","identifier":[{"id":"85089247325","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-755e2f8d-0d51-49a6-bede-39c2148cda66"],"keyword":["business travel","economics","network analysis","tacit knowledge"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-863EAD4578FCEDB38C248CD34B884DA3"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85337687/knowhow_index_NHB_cameraready.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"harvard university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Harvard University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michele","key":"cosciapau","last":"Coscia","locid":"90b501a9-4d44-460b-b193-4d7d524bb21b","name":"Coscia, Michele","orcid":"0000-0001-5984-5137","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Frank","key":"neffkepau","last":"Neffke","name":"Neffke, Frank","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Ricardo","key":"hausmannpau","last":"Hausmann","name":"Hausmann, Ricardo","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/s41562-020-0922-x","issn":["23973374"],"journal":"Nature Human Behaviour","pages":"1-10","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"Nature Human Behaviour","title_org":"Nature Human Behavior"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85089247325"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-755e2f8d-0d51-49a6-bede-39c2148cda66"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Knowledge diffusion in the network of international business travel","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382998","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Many people view news on social media, yet the production of news items online has come under fire because of the common spreading of misinformation. Social media platforms police their content in various ways. Primarily they rely on crowdsourced ‘flags’: users signal to the platform that a specific news item might be misleading and, if they raise enough of them, the item will be fact-checked. However, real-world data show that the most flagged news sources are also the most popular and—supposedly—reliable ones. In this paper, we show that this phenomenon can be explained by the unreasonable assumptions that current content policing strategies make about how the online social media environment is shaped. The most realistic assumption is that confirmation bias will prevent a user from flagging a news item if they share the same political bias as the news source producing it. We show, via agent-based simulations, that a model reproducing our current understanding of the social media environment will necessarily result in the most neutral and accurate sources receiving most flags.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Coscia, Michele","Rossi, Luca"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381620"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Fake news","Social media","Social networks","content policing","echo chambers","flagging"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"distortions of political bias in crowdsourced misinformation flagging","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1098/rsif.2020.0020","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381620","identifier":[{"id":"85086354599","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-17547d2b-f67e-4edd-933a-7363d94bcecf"],"keyword":["content policing","echo chambers","fake news","flagging","social media","social networks"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-1BF8BD138D69D3CEE8B86F9894E54FB2"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85143315/rsif.2020.0020.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1098/rsif.2020.0020","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michele","key":"cosciapau","last":"Coscia","locid":"90b501a9-4d44-460b-b193-4d7d524bb21b","name":"Coscia, Michele","orcid":"0000-0001-5984-5137","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Luca","key":"rossipau","last":"Rossi","locid":"1b45e3f5-b8cd-4c0d-a444-2901304f76ee","name":"Rossi, Luca","orcid":"0000-0002-3629-2039","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1098/rsif.2020.0020","issn":["17425662","17425689"],"issn_org":["17425689"],"issue":"167","journal":"Journal of the Royal Society. Interface","status":"p","title":"Journal of the Royal Society. Interface","volume":"17"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85086354599"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-17547d2b-f67e-4edd-933a-7363d94bcecf"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Distortions of political bias in crowdsourced misinformation flagging","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381620","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Estimating the distance covered by a propagation phenomenon on a network is an important task: it can help us estimating the infectiousness of a disease or the effectiveness of an online viral marketing campaign. However, so far the only way to make such an estimate relies on solving the optimal transportation problem, or by adapting graph signal processing techniques. Such solutions are either inefficient, because they require solving a complex optimization problem; or fragile, because they were not designed with this problem in mind. In this paper, we propose a new generalized Euclidean approach to estimate distances between weighted groups of nodes in a network. We do so by adapting the Mahalanobis distance, incorporating the graph's topology via the pseudoinverse of its Laplacian. In experiments we see that this measure returns intuitive distances which agree with the ones a human would estimate. We also show that the measure is able to recover the infection parameter in an epidemic model, or the activation threshold in a cascade model. We conclude by showing that the measure can be used in online social media settings to identify fast-spreading behaviors. Our measure is also less computationally expensive.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Coscia, Michele"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382062"],"event":{"acronym":"ICWSM","title":"International AAAI Conference on Web and Social Media"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Complex networks","Euclidean distance","Social networks","network distance"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"generalized euclidean measure to estimate network distances","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85143383/7284_Article_Text_10514_1_10_20200601.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382062","identifier":[{"id":"85099545775","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-35f62821-0c48-428f-8dcb-931b072f8033"],"keyword":["complex networks","euclidean distance","network distance","social networks"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-A2AA57225BA5EAB5ABCA9E9488EC2BAE"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.aaai.org/ojs/index.php/ICWSM/article/view/7284","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85143383/7284_Article_Text_10514_1_10_20200601.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michele","key":"cosciapau","last":"Coscia","locid":"90b501a9-4d44-460b-b193-4d7d524bb21b","name":"Coscia, Michele","orcid":"0000-0001-5984-5137","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"pages":"119-129","publisher":"AAAI Press","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the International AAAI Conference on Web and Social Media"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-35f62821-0c48-428f-8dcb-931b072f8033"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Generalized Euclidean Measure to Estimate Network Distances","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382062","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"We describe a problem in complex networks we call the Node Vector Distance (NVD) problem, and we survey algorithms currently able to address it. Complex networks are a useful tool to map a non-trivial set of relationships among connected entities, or nodes. An agent—e.g., a disease—can occupy multiple nodes at the same time and can spread through the edges. The node vector distance problem is to estimate the distance traveled by the agent between two moments in time. This is closely related to the Optimal Transportation Problem (OTP), which has received attention in fields such as computer vision. OTP solutions can be used to solve the node vector distance problem, but they are not the only valid approaches. Here, we examine four classes of solutions, showing their differences and similarities both on synthetic networks and real world network data. The NVD problem has a much wider applicability than computer vision, being related to problems in economics, epidemiology, viral marketing, and sociology, to cite a few. We show how solutions to the NVD problem have a wide range of applications, and we provide a roadmap to general and computationally tractable solutions. We have implemented all methods presented in this article in a publicly available open source library, which can be used for result replication.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Coscia, Michele","Gomez-Lievano, Andres","McNerney, James","Neffke, Frank"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383240"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Analysis Group, Inc.","Harvard University"],"keyword":["Complex networks","Distance measure","Social networks"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the node vector distance problem in complex networks","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85576246/main.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383240","identifier":[{"id":"85100809550","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-862316d0-114d-4d6a-bc20-89d5bde3ede7"],"keyword":["complex networks","distance measure","social networks"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-7E32BFE5C7FFB8451EB6C9FE57BAAD29"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85576246/main.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"analysis group inc","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Analysis Group, Inc.","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"us","key":"harvard university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Harvard University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michele","key":"cosciapau","last":"Coscia","locid":"90b501a9-4d44-460b-b193-4d7d524bb21b","name":"Coscia, Michele","orcid":"0000-0001-5984-5137","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"gomezlievanopau","last":"Gomez-Lievano","name":"Gomez-Lievano, Andres","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"James","key":"mcnerneypau","last":"McNerney","name":"McNerney, James","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Frank","key":"neffkepau","last":"Neffke","name":"Neffke, Frank","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3416509","issn":["03600300","15577341"],"issn_org":["03600300"],"issue":"6","journal":"ACM Computing Surveys","paperid":"124","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"ACM Computing Surveys","volume":"53"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85100809550"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-862316d0-114d-4d6a-bc20-89d5bde3ede7"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The Node Vector Distance Problem in Complex Networks","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383240","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Human action recognition has recently started to find its way into applications in different applications. Accordingly, human action recognition methods are becoming increasingly important in our daily life. They are used for different purposes such as automation, security, surveillance, health, smart home systems, and customer behaviour prediction, among others. Though have more systems with methods provides a rich pool of choices, it is important to well understand the performance of these systems and their success rates in recognizing the right activities in order to decide on the most appropriate system for the current application domain. This survey tackles this issue by analyzing and commenting on the available human action recognition systems and methods.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Ozyer, Tansel","Selin, Ak Duygu","Alhajj, Reda"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-631834"],"event":{"acronym":"ASONAM","end":"2020-12-10","place":"Virtual, Online, nl","start":"2020-12-07","title":"12th IEEE/ACM International Conference on Advances in Social Networks Analysis and Mining, ASONAM 2020","year":"2020"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, Middle East","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Alhajj, Reda"],"country":["Turkey"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","TOBB University of Economics and Technology"],"keyword":["Human activity recognition"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"recent trends in emotion analysis a big data analysis perspective","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85103696323","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-631834","identifier":[{"id":"85103696323","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-cca15a94-09ca-48ec-94a0-d13fc79d2cdc"],"keyword":["Human activity recognition","skeleton based recognition","video based recognition"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-BACB063F0E038FAA9CF783F768AF97CE"],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"tr","key":"tobb university of economics and technology","loc":["Turkey","Asia, Middle East","OECD"],"name":"TOBB University of Economics and Technology","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Engineering, The Maersk Mc-Kinney Moller Institute","SDU, The Faculty of Engineering, The Maersk Mc-Kinney Moller Institute, SDU Health Informatics and Technology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Tansel","key":"ozyerpau","last":"Ozyer","name":"Ozyer, Tansel","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ak Duygu","key":"selinpau","last":"Selin","name":"Selin, Ak Duygu","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Reda","key":"alhajjpau","last":"Alhajj","locid":"1bf434d6-4241-4d51-9b4f-8537145d5938","name":"Alhajj, Reda","orcid":"0000-0001-6657-9738","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Martin","key":"atzmullerpedh","last":"Atzmuller","name":"Atzmuller, Martin","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Michele","key":"cosciapedh","last":"Coscia","name":"Coscia, Michele","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Rokia","key":"missaouipedh","last":"Missaoui","name":"Missaoui, Rokia","rank":6,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/asonam49781.2020.9381441","eisbn":["9781728110561"],"issn":["24739928"],"pages":"710-714","publisher":"IEEE","series":"Proceedings - IEEE/ACM International Conference on Advances in Social Network Analysis and Mining, ASONAM","status":"p","title":"2020 IEEE/ACM International Conference on Advances in Social Networks Analysis and Mining (ASONAM)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85103696323"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-cca15a94-09ca-48ec-94a0-d13fc79d2cdc"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Recent Trends in Emotion Analysis : A Big Data Analysis Perspective","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-631834","year":"2020","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Bipartite networks are a well known strategy to study a variety of phenomena. The commonly used method to deal with this type of network is to project the bipartite data into a unipartite weighted graph and then using a backboning technique to extract only the meaningful edges. Despite the wide availability of different methods both for projection and backboning, we believe that there has been little attention to the effect that the combination of these two processes has on the data and on the resulting network topology. In this paper we study the effect that the possible combinations of projection and backboning techniques have on a bipartite network. We show that the 12 methods group into two clusters producing unipartite networks with very different topologies. We also show that the resulting level of network centralization is highly affected by the combination of projection and backboning applied.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Coscia, Michele","Rossi, Luca"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382397"],"event":{"acronym":"ASONAM","title":"IEEE/ACM International Conference on Advances in Social Networks Analysis and Mining"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Complex networks","network backboning"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the impact of projection and backboning on network topologies","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3341161.3342862","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382397","identifier":[{"id":"85078831994","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-4b76fefe-7068-423c-97bf-4bace068e6c2"],"keyword":["complex networks","network backboning"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-B1E769A7A6687645072673D024FD4C32"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84366334/The_Impact_of_Projection_and_Backboning_on_Network_Topologies.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3341161.3342862","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michele","key":"cosciapau","last":"Coscia","locid":"90b501a9-4d44-460b-b193-4d7d524bb21b","name":"Coscia, Michele","orcid":"0000-0001-5984-5137","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Luca","key":"rossipau","last":"Rossi","locid":"1b45e3f5-b8cd-4c0d-a444-2901304f76ee","name":"Rossi, Luca","orcid":"0000-0002-3629-2039","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3341161.3342862","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"International Conference on Advances in Social Networks Analysis and Mining"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85078831994"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-4b76fefe-7068-423c-97bf-4bace068e6c2"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The Impact of Projection and Backboning on Network Topologies","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382397","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Discovering communities in complex networks means grouping nodes similar to each other, to uncover latent information about them. There are hundreds of different algorithms to solve the community detection task, each with its own understanding and definition of what a \"community\" is. Dozens of review works attempt to order such a diverse landscape -- classifying community discovery algorithms by the process they employ to detect communities, by their explicitly stated definition of community, or by their performance on a standardized task. In this paper, we classify community discovery algorithms according to a fourth criterion: the similarity of their results. We create an Algorithm Similarity Network (ASN), whose nodes are the community detection approaches, connected if they return similar groupings. We then perform community detection on this network, grouping algorithms that consistently return the same partitions or overlapping coverage over a span of more than one thousand synthetic and real world networks. This paper is an attempt to create a similarity-based classification of community detection algorithms based on empirical data. It improves over the state of the art by comparing more than seventy approaches, discovering that the ASN contains well-separated groups, making it a sensible tool for practitioners, aiding their choice of algorithms fitting their analytic needs.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Coscia, Michele"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383541"],"event":{"acronym":"ASONAM","title":"IEEE/ACM International Conference on Advances in Social Networks Analysis and Mining"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Cluster Analysis","Complex networks","Social networks","community discovery"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"discovering communities of community discovery","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3341161.3342860","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383541","identifier":[{"id":"85078852054","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-9bc6ee36-77a2-4ce4-9e5c-5b11a8407447"],"keyword":["Cluster analysis","community discovery","complex networks","social networks"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-BFBF590097C3C2003796D5FEB3F926B5"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84366317/cd_of_cd.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3341161.3342860","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michele","key":"cosciapau","last":"Coscia","locid":"90b501a9-4d44-460b-b193-4d7d524bb21b","name":"Coscia, Michele","orcid":"0000-0001-5984-5137","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3341161.3342860","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"International Conference on Advances in Social Networks Analysis and Mining"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85078852054"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-9bc6ee36-77a2-4ce4-9e5c-5b11a8407447"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Discovering Communities of Community Discovery","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383541","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"The global trade system can be viewed as a dynamic ecosystem in which exporters struggle for resources: the markets in which they export. We can think that the aim of an exporter is to gain the entirety of a market share (say, car imports from the United States). This is similar to the objective of an organism in its attempt to monopolize a given subset of resources in an ecosystem. In this paper, we adopt a multilayer network approach to describe this struggle. We use longitudinal, multiplex data on trade relations, spanning several decades. We connect two countries with a directed link if the source country’s appearance in a market correlates with the target country’s disappearing, where a market is defined as a country-product combination in a given decade. Each market is a layer in the network. We show that, by analyzing the countries’ network roles in each layer, we are able to classify them as out-competing, transitioning or displaced. This classification is a meaningful one: when testing the future export patterns of these countries, we show that out-competing countries have distinctly stronger growth rates than the other two classes.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Viña-Cervantes, Viviana","Coscia, Michele","Lambiotte, Renaud"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384943"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["Belgium","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Namur","University of Oxford"],"keyword":["Complex networks","Macroeconomics","Trade"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the struggle for existence in the world market ecosystem","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0203915","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384943","identifier":[{"id":"85054464008","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-f9721c04-faa0-4648-8553-2fe7375e53e7"],"keyword":["complex networks","macroeconomics","trade"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-5D397B6DA6FA086CEE22B59EA55FF395"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83422072/journal.pone.0203915.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0203915","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2018","yearSub":"2018"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"be","key":"university of namur","loc":["Belgium","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Namur","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"gb","key":"university of oxford","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Oxford","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Viviana","key":"viñacervantespau","last":"Viña-Cervantes","name":"Viña-Cervantes, Viviana","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Michele","key":"cosciapau","last":"Coscia","locid":"90b501a9-4d44-460b-b193-4d7d524bb21b","name":"Coscia, Michele","orcid":"0000-0001-5984-5137","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Renaud","key":"lambiottepau","last":"Lambiotte","name":"Lambiotte, Renaud","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1371/journal.pone.0203915","issn":["19326203"],"journal":"PLOS ONE","publisher":"Public Library of Science","status":"p","title":"PLOS ONE"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85054464008"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-f9721c04-faa0-4648-8553-2fe7375e53e7"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The struggle for existence in the world market ecosystem","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384943","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"Recent advances in mass spectrometry (MS)-based technologies are now set to transform translational cancer proteomics from an idea to a practice. Here, we present a robust proteomic workflow for the analysis of clinically relevant human cancer tissues that allows quantitation of thousands of tumor proteins in several hours of measuring time and a total turnaround of a few days. We applied it to a chemorefractory metastatic case of the extremely rare urachal carcinoma. Quantitative comparison of lung metastases and surrounding tissue revealed several significantly upregulated proteins, among them lysine-specific histone demethylase 1 (LSD1/KDM1A). LSD1 is an epigenetic regulator and the target of active development efforts in oncology. Thus, clinical cancer proteomics can rapidly and efficiently identify actionable therapeutic options. While currently described for a single case study, we envision that it can be applied broadly to other patients in a similar condition.","author_count":11,"contributors":["Doll, Sophia","Kriegmair, Maximilian C.","Santos, Alberto","Wierer, Michael","Coscia, Fabian","Neil, Helen Michele","Porubsky, Stefan","Geyer, Philipp E.","Mund, Andreas","Nuhn, Philipp","Mann, Matthias"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-413107"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Max Planck Institute of Biochemistry"],"keyword":["Case study","Clinical proteomics","Epigenetics","Mass spectrometry"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"rapid proteomic analysis for solid tumors reveals lsd000000000001 as a drug target in an end stage cancer patient","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/1878-0261.12326","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-413107","identifier":[{"id":"29901861","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85050957897","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC6068348","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["ku-20f8925e-5a27-4ce2-807f-800163f3d584"],"keyword":["case study","clinical proteomics","epigenetics","mass spectrometry","urachal carcinoma"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-A2C60449F321F205DABA26D98A4F5FC8"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/1878-0261.12326","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/209322728/Doll_et_al_2018_Molecular_Oncology.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2018","yearSub":"2018"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Novo Nordisk Foundation Center for Protein Research, Novo Nordisk Foundation Center for Protein Research"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"max planck institute of biochemistry","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Max Planck Institute of Biochemistry","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","country":"dk","first":"Sophia","key":"dollpau","last":"Doll","locid":"b65237bb-c5a2-41d7-87ca-d6be89cb4b26","name":"Doll, Sophia","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Maximilian C.","key":"kriegmairpau","last":"Kriegmair","name":"Kriegmair, Maximilian C.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Alberto","key":"santospau","last":"Santos","locid":"75866a6a-0c79-4882-b550-a6c550000e21","name":"Santos, Alberto","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Michael","key":"wiererpau","last":"Wierer","name":"Wierer, Michael","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 1","first":"Fabian","key":"cosciapau","last":"Coscia","locid":"a95a0078-640b-4ef4-9f07-15ee9bb03fe7","name":"Coscia, Fabian","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Helen Michele","key":"neilpau","last":"Neil","locid":"4a3eb0f5-17d9-4bd8-a6d7-d3f963abf923","name":"Neil, Helen Michele","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Stefan","key":"porubskypau","last":"Porubsky","name":"Porubsky, Stefan","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 1","first":"Philipp E.","key":"geyerpau","last":"Geyer","locid":"fc987200-bf00-477b-8c57-e83a7984b89e","name":"Geyer, Philipp E.","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Andreas","key":"mundpau","last":"Mund","locid":"d0229dc7-e408-4e09-a414-06a2226c6bc0","name":"Mund, Andreas","orcid":"0000-0002-7843-5341","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Philipp","key":"nuhnpau","last":"Nuhn","name":"Nuhn, Philipp","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 1","first":"Matthias","key":"mannpau","last":"Mann","locid":"f463f571-68c5-44fb-bff1-bb9d748fcda8","name":"Mann, Matthias","orcid":"0000-0003-1292-4799","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["29901861"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1002/1878-0261.12326","issn":["15747891","18780261"],"issn_org":["15747891"],"issue":"8","journal":"Molecular Oncology","pages":"1296-1307","status":"p","title":"Molecular Oncology","volume":"12"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85050957897"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-20f8925e-5a27-4ce2-807f-800163f3d584"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Rapid proteomic analysis for solid tumors reveals LSD1 as a drug target in an end-stage cancer patient","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-413107","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"Governments in modern societies undertake an array of complex functions that shape politics and economics, individual and group behavior, and the natural, social, and built environment. How are governments structured to execute these diverse responsibilities? How do those structures vary, and what explains the differences? To examine these longstanding questions, we develop a technique for mapping Internet “footprint” of government with network science methods. We use this approach to describe and analyze the diversity in functional scale and structure among the 50 US state governments reflected in the webpages and links they have created online: 32.5 million webpages and 110 million hyperlinks among 47,631 agencies. We first verify that this extensive online footprint systematically reflects known characteristics: 50 hierarchically organized networks of state agencies that scale with population and are specialized around easily identifiable functions in accordance with legal mandates. We also find that the footprint reflects extensive diversity among these state functional hierarchies. We hypothesize that this variation should reflect, among other factors, state income, economic structure, ideology, and location. We find that government structures are most strongly associated with state economic structures, with location and income playing more limited roles. Voters’ recent ideological preferences about the proper roles and extent of government are not significantly associated with the scale and structure of their state governments as reflected online. We conclude that the online footprint of governments offers a broad and comprehensive window on how they are structured that can help deepen understanding of those structures.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Kosack, Stephen","Coscia, Michele","Smith, Evann","Albrecht, Kim","Barabasi, Albert-Laszlo","Hausmann, Ricardo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383619"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Harvard University","Northeastern University","University of Washington"],"keyword":["Complex networks","Government","Political economy","Web crawling"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"functional structures of us state governments","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1803228115","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383619","identifier":[{"id":"85056562479","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-a1c26168-2dab-423d-b2ef-fa52b7cf5c6d"],"keyword":["complex networks","government","political economy","web crawling"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-96DED0C382A09244005D887B6D83D62D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83497809/1803228115.full.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1803228115","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2018","yearSub":"2018"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"us","key":"university of washington","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Washington","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"us","key":"harvard university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Harvard University","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"us","key":"northeastern university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Northeastern University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Stephen","key":"kosackpau","last":"Kosack","name":"Kosack, Stephen","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Michele","key":"cosciapau","last":"Coscia","locid":"90b501a9-4d44-460b-b193-4d7d524bb21b","name":"Coscia, Michele","orcid":"0000-0001-5984-5137","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Evann","key":"smithpau","last":"Smith","name":"Smith, Evann","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Kim","key":"albrechtpau","last":"Albrecht","name":"Albrecht, Kim","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Albert-Laszlo","key":"barabasipau","last":"Barabasi","name":"Barabasi, Albert-Laszlo","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Ricardo","key":"hausmannpau","last":"Hausmann","name":"Hausmann, Ricardo","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1073/pnas.1803228115","issn":["00278424","10916490"],"issn_org":["00278424"],"journal":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America","publisher":"The National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85056562479"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-a1c26168-2dab-423d-b2ef-fa52b7cf5c6d"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Functional structures of US state governments","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383619","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"Online social media contain valuable quantitative and qualitative data, necessary to advance complex social systems studies. However, these data vaults are often behind a wall: the owners of the media sites dictate what, when, and how much data can be collected via a mandatory interface (called Application Program Interface: API). To work with such restrictions, network scientists have designed sampling methods, which do not require a full crawl of the data to obtain a representative picture of the underlying social network. However, such sampling methods are usually evaluated only on one dimension: what strategy allows for the extraction of a sample whose statistical properties are closest to the original network? In this paper we go beyond this view, by creating a benchmark that tests the performance of a method in a multifaceted way. When evaluating a network sampling algorithm, we take into account the API policies and the budget a researcher has to explore the network. By doing so, we show that some methods which are considered to perform poorly actually can perform well with tighter budgets, or with different API policies. Our results show that the decision of which sampling algorithm to use is not monodimensional. It is not enough to ask which method returns the most accurate sample, one has also to consider through which API constraints it has to go, and how much it can spend on the crawl.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Coscia, Michele","Rossi, Luca"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382154"],"event":{"end":"2018-12-13","place":"Seattle, us","start":"2018-12-10","title":"2018 IEEE International Conference on Big Data (Big Data)","year":"2018"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Network analysis","Social media","network sampling"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"benchmarking api costs of network sampling strategies","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83565384/network_sampling_final.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382154","identifier":[{"id":"85062609608","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-3c8b5a94-25be-4acb-b274-db7f30399704"],"keyword":["Social media","network analysis","network sampling"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-F2E79B4449B0D004226304DD9952ADD2"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83565384/network_sampling_final.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michele","key":"cosciapau","last":"Coscia","locid":"90b501a9-4d44-460b-b193-4d7d524bb21b","name":"Coscia, Michele","orcid":"0000-0001-5984-5137","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Luca","key":"rossipau","last":"Rossi","locid":"1b45e3f5-b8cd-4c0d-a444-2901304f76ee","name":"Rossi, Luca","orcid":"0000-0002-3629-2039","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/bigdata.2018.8622486","eisbn":["9781538650356"],"isbn":["9781538650349"],"publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"2018 IEEE International Conference on Big Data, BigData 2018"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85062609608"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-3c8b5a94-25be-4acb-b274-db7f30399704"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Benchmarking API Costs of Network Sampling Strategies","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382154","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
